# Docs — etude-9uf.5

The proof run's whole output is knowledge, so the docs phase is where it becomes
usable. Everything below went into `docs/plans/dogfood/supervisor-runbook.md` —
the document a supervisor actually follows — rather than into a plan note.

## Three sections added to the supervisor runbook (+53 lines)

**1. "Run the gate from the WORKER'S worktree, not your own."** Placed inside the
per-phase loop, immediately before the exit-code handling, because that is the
moment a supervisor decides where to type the command. It states the failure in
the form that matters — *every other gate defect announces itself with a BLOCK;
this one announces itself with a pass* — gives the literal `cd <lane> && …`
invocation, and records that two of five gates on this very run were invoked from
the wrong worktree before it was noticed. Points at `etude-4qi`.

**2. "Append the verdict BEFORE you act on it."** Carries the measurement:
`etude gate` alone passed **0 of its 5 attempts** — on `refs/etude/runs/etude-3xt`,
which the text now names. Most usefully it documents the **fingerprint** — a gap
in a phase's round sequence is an attempt whose verdict was appended late or
never. Three exist in this epic (`.3` verify r2, `.3` docs r2, `.4` verify r2) —
plus a FOURTH that is a false positive, which is the more useful half of the
finding: see below. It also warns that a bootstrap panel re-records seats
evidence-free and omits the deterministic check seats, so a reader auditing "did
the checks pass" must read the ESCALATED attempt rather than the passing one.

**3. "Do not edit an artifact after its gate passes."** The drift found on this
run: fold before the gate, or capture a rerun stage and gate again. Points at
`etude-pq7`.

**Corrected at the docs gate, and worth recording.** The first draft attributed
BOTH the "0 of 5" and the "two of five gates from the wrong worktree" figures to
`etude-9uf.5`. They are measurements of `etude-3xt`. `.5`'s own run has three
`etude gate` attempts, all from the correct worktree — so "of five" meant two
different runs within thirty lines, in a section whose entire point is that a
claim must be auditable against the record.

The draft also promised the round-gap fingerprint "would have caught what six
separate seats had to notice". The manifests hold **three** gaps, carrying at
most five seat verdicts — so either the count was wrong or a missed verdict left
no gap, in which case the arithmetic would not have caught it. The text now says
**partial** and explains why: a verdict never assigned a round id leaves no hole,
so a clean gap check is not proof the record is complete. Promising a detector
more completeness than it has is how the next person stops looking.

**Round 2 then corrected the corrections, twice, and both matter.**

First, the drift sentence was ALSO `etude-3xt`'s measurement — round 1 had
asserted it "IS about `.5`" and I folded that premise without checking it.
`etude-3xt` has five gated artifacts and two drifted; `.5` has three that passed
a gate, and two of those drifted. Both are true; the denominators differ, and I
had published one run's numerator against the other's run name.

Second, and more valuable: **the detector has a FALSE POSITIVE mode, and this
epic's own record contains one.** `etude-9uf.1`'s plan rounds run 1, 3, 4 — a
hole by the stated rule — but that run has a STAGE named `plan.r2` which gates
`plan.r3` and `plan.r4` both review, so the counter skipped the number to avoid a
collision. No verdict is missing. I had told the reader the detector's only
weakness was false negatives; a supervisor running it as first described would
get four hits with no guidance on the fourth. The runbook now states both failure
modes and says to treat a hit as a question.

Filed `etude-a1i` (and corrected, since it carried the wrong count too): the
runbook describes a detector nothing implements — the
documented-but-unimplemented gap this epic exists to close, recreated in the act
of documenting it.

**Also corrected: a section this epic's own work had made stale.** The
seat-exception text still said the exception "lives in prose, not in code" and
that `etude gate` "resolves the `invoke` string verbatim". Bead `etude-5f6`
landed the fallback ladder in `17d0ab5`, so the exception IS encoded and the
ladder IS walked. The observed outcome is unchanged — every gate still escalates
— but the stated mechanism was wrong, and the precise gap is narrower than the
old text claimed: `etude gate` cannot CONSUME an in-harness verdict.

## Why the runbook and not elsewhere

`etude-3xt`'s own docs gate surfaced the general form of this: that bead's
practice fix landed in `retro-ledger.md` while `supervisor-runbook.md` step 6
still described the old two-step habit — the fix documented somewhere other than
where the reader looks. All three sections above are placed at the point of use:
inside the per-phase loop, not in an appendix.

## What is NOT changed

- **`.etude/workflow.yaml`, `.etude/registry.yaml`** — the tiers and seats are
  right; the gap is that `etude gate` cannot consume an in-harness verdict, which
  is code (`etude-4ed`), not config.
- **`docs/plans/dogfood/review-gate-runbook.md`** — covers seat conduct and the
  degraded-gate policy, both unchanged by this run.
- **`docs/retro.md`** — `etude-3xt` updated it in the same window; nothing here
  touches retro capture.
- **No new document.** The findings belong in the runbook that already exists.
  A "proof run findings" page is a page nobody opens twice.

## Findings that became beads instead of prose

Documentation cannot fix a missing mechanism, so four went to the tracker:

| Bead | |
|---|---|
| `etude-4qi` (P1) | gate checks run in the caller's cwd; the record cannot be audited for it |
| `etude-pq7` (P2) | no detection for a stage artifact edited after its gate |
| `etude-4ed` (extended) | verdict injection must COMPLETE an attempt, not re-record it lossily |
| `etude-fn8` (P3) | mkdocs nav points at a file bead `.2` deleted — a docs defect four docs gates missed, because neither `docs-check` nor `docs-reality` builds the site |
| `etude-a1i` (P2) | implement the round-gap check the runbook now describes |

## Checks

`make docs-check` and `make docs-reality` green.

Worth stating plainly, since this bead is about what the model can and cannot
see: **neither reads prose, and neither builds the site.** They are why
`etude-fn8` survived four docs gates on this epic. The only thing that has
reliably caught a false sentence here is a seat reading it.
